This empty `catch {}` block silently swallows all errors from `setupWatchers`, including unexpected failures in `getGitDir()`, `watchFile()`, or `configureBranchWatcher()`. The project rule is explicit: catch blocks must never be empty—even for opportunistic/best-effort code paths. Errors should be logged at minimum with `logger.debug` from `storybook/internal/node-logger`. The comment explains the rationale but does not satisfy the logging requirement. Example fix:
```ts
} catch (error) {
  logger.debug(`Git state watcher setup failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}`);
}
```

The `.catch()` handler in `reconfigureBranchWatcher` discards the caught error entirely. If `configureBranchWatcher` fails for an unexpected reason (e.g., a non-ENOENT I/O error from `readHeadRef`), there is no diagnostic signal—only a silent watcher rebuild. Per project rules, errors must be logged at debug level before continuing. Example fix:
```ts
void this.configureBranchWatcher(gitDir, generation).catch((error) => {
  logger.debug(`Branch watcher reconfiguration failed: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}`);
  if (this.isWatcherSetupCurrent(generation)) {
    this.scheduleWatcherRebuild();
  }
});
```

`FSWatcher` and `watch` are both exported from `'node:fs'` but appear in two separate `import type` lines (lines 1 and 3). These should be merged into a single statement:
```ts
import type { FSWatcher, watch } from 'node:fs';
import type { readFile, stat } from 'node:fs/promises';
```
